@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — modern claims that circulate as old ones
#
# The first file in this directory, and the reason the directory exists. What
# is held here orders no tradition and witnesses none; it begins with a named
# author in a datable work and is afterwards reported as ancient. That is the
# kind of node SPEC §0 calls the most valuable, because it removes a gate
# nobody else is removing.
#

pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The ranking of emotions by measured level"@en, "Die Ordnung der Affekte nach gemessener Stufe"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that emotional and mental states occupy fixed positions on a single numbered scale, ascending from shame through fear, anger and pride to reason, love, joy, peace and enlightenment, and that the position can be established by testing."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "David R. Hawkins" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A perennial map of consciousness, reported as the distilled agreement of the mystical traditions." ;
    skos:note "An earlier form of this note held that no transmission ranks affects on a single ordered scale. That was withdrawn: the ground for it had been a search across the holdings of this graph, which says what has been entered here and not what the transmissions say. The search that had not been carried out finds one at once. Rūpa Gosvāmin ranks the five primary forms of devotional love in a single ascending order in the sixteenth century, in the verse itself, and traditions/gaudiya-vaisnava.ttl now holds it. The finding of this node is therefore not that ranking affects is modern. It is narrower and survives: the particular scale below, with its count, its order and its claim of measurability, begins here."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest older relative is pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling, and the comparison sharpens rather than dissolves this node. That transmission supplies the vibration vocabulary and an axis of self-reference on which a feeling rises or falls, and it was read at the wording for this purpose. It supplies no ranking of unlike feelings against one another. The single numbered order is therefore not inherited, and the question of where it does come from is open in prima-materia#12."@en ;
    skos:note "The diagnostic markers, given so that any later list presenting itself as old can be measured against a dated first appearance. The count: seventeen steps. The order of the lower band: shame, guilt, apathy, grief, fear, desire, anger, pride. The turn at the middle: courage, then neutrality, then willingness in eleventh place. The upper band: acceptance, reason, love, joy, peace, enlightenment. A list that shares the count, the relative order of shame, guilt, fear and anger, and the eleventh position of willingness has not arrived at them independently."@en ;
    dcterms:date "1995" ;
    dcterms:source "D. R. Hawkins, Power vs. Force (1995)" .

pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The reading of feeling from colour and vibration"@en, "Die Lesung des Gefühls aus Farbe und Schwingung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that every feeling has a colour, that the colour can be read off a body of subtle matter surrounding the person, and that the brightness of the colour measures the strength of the feeling."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. W. Leadbeater; Annie Besant" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "An ancient science of the aura, reported as eastern in origin and as the common property of the older traditions." ;
    skos:note "Opened for prima-materia#361, on the suspicion that the modern one-dimensional ranking of emotions begins here. Read at the wording. The suspicion is only half borne out, and the half that fails is the more interesting one."@en ;
    skos:note "What is here: a fixed key from feeling to colour, given as a list of the commonest hues; brightness and depth of colour stated outright as a measure of the strength and activity of the feeling; the vocabulary of vibration throughout; and an axis of self-reference applied to every colour alike, on which a thought with something of self in it turns downwards and one without turns upwards. The axis is the part that later scales inherit."@en ;
    skos:note "What is not here, and the point of the node: a ranking of the feelings against one another. The plate is a grid of twenty-five fields, not a series, and the chapter introduces its colours as a list of the commonest ones. Where the text does rank, it ranks within a single colour by purity and luminosity, so that the same feeling has a lower and a higher form. That is a second dimension and not a scale. A later list that puts unlike feelings in one numbered order therefore does not have it from here, and prima-materia#12 cannot be answered by pointing at this node."@en ;
    dcterms:date "1902" ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, Man Visible and Invisible (London: Theosophical Publishing Society, 1902), the frontispiece plate and the chapter Colours and their Meaning, pp. 81-82" .

pmc:HicksEmotionalGuidanceScale a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The emotional guidance scale"@en, "Die Skala der emotionalen Führung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that emotions form a graded ladder that indicates how near a person stands to a desired state, with joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love and appreciation at its top rung."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "Esther Hicks and Jerry Hicks" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A law of the emotions, presented as a description of how feeling works rather than as one author's arrangement." ;
    skos:note "The upper block of this scale reappears almost word for word in later lists that present themselves as older. Recorded so that the wording has a dated first appearance to be checked against."@en ;
    skos:note "The diagnostic marker is the top rung and its sequence: joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love, appreciation. Six terms, and the fourth of them is the one that gives the sequence away, because a list built from an older stock has no reason to place freedom between empowerment and love. A later list carrying this block, in this order, carries it from here."@en ;
    dcterms:date "2004" ;
    dcterms:source "E. Hicks and J. Hicks, Ask and It Is Given (2004), ch. 22" .
